From 365656c0a80b46eac06dded176375bcce9bfc1d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yi chen <94xhn1@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 00:51:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tcp): poll pending close on LAST_ACK completion to avoid
 lost wakeup

Fixes espressif/esp-lwip#84.

tcp_process()'s FIN_WAIT_1->TIME_WAIT, FIN_WAIT_2->TIME_WAIT and
CLOSING->TIME_WAIT transitions all call TCP_POLL_LINGERING_CLOSE(pcb)
(added by 88136ab1, "tcp_in: Fix incomplete closure if linger active")
right after the transition completes, so that a netconn waiting in
lwip_netconn_do_close_internal() (registered via tcp_poll() while the
close was still pending) gets a synchronous chance to notice completion.

The LAST_ACK -> TF_CLOSED transition was missing the same call. A
FIN-only segment always has tcp_seg::len == 0 (see the
"seg->len == 0" assert in tcp_enqueue_flags()), so when the final ACK
only acknowledges our own FIN, recv_acked stays 0 and the TCP_EVENT_SENT
sent-callback in tcp_input() is never invoked either. If the netconn
already fully shut down its rx side (TF_RXCLOSED, unconditionally set by
tcp_close()), tcp_input_delayed_close() also skips TCP_EVENT_ERR before
freeing the pcb. With no callback firing at all, a task blocked in
close() on that connection is never woken - a permanent hang, not just a
leak - matching the "Case C" packet trace in #84 (remote closes first,
local side has unacked data but no unsent data left when the final ACK
arrives).

Add TCP_POLL_LINGERING_CLOSE(pcb) to the LAST_ACK case as well, mirroring
the other three transitions, so pending close completion is checked
before the pcb is freed.

Verified with a standalone reproduction that extracts the exact
TCP_EVENT_POLL / TCP_POLL_LINGERING_CLOSE macros and the CLOSING vs.
LAST_ACK switch-case logic from this tree: before this change the CLOSING
path fires the poll/close-completion callback (1 call, semaphore posted)
while the LAST_ACK path fires 0 calls before the pcb is freed
(lost wakeup); after adding the same macro call to LAST_ACK, both paths
fire the callback before free. Also syntax/type-checked the real edit
in-tree against src/core/tcp_in.c with contrib/ports/unix headers
(gcc -fsyntax-only -Wall -Wextra, both default opts and
-DESP_LWIP=1 -DLWIP_SO_LINGER=1), no warnings.

I used generative AI tools (Claude Code) to research, diagnose and draft
this change; I reviewed the reasoning and the resulting diff and take
responsibility for both.

Signed-off-by: yi chen <94xhn1@gmail.com>
---
 src/core/tcp_in.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/core/tcp_in.c b/src/core/tcp_in.c
index 9af29630..fbbd34ce 100644
--- a/src/core/tcp_in.c
+++ b/src/core/tcp_in.c
@@ -1062,6 +1062,18 @@ tcp_process(struct tcp_pcb *pcb)
         LWIP_DEBUGF(TCP_DEBUG, ("TCP connection closed: LAST_ACK %"U16_F" -> %"U16_F".\n", inseg.tcphdr->src, inseg.tcphdr->dest));
         /* bugfix #21699: don't set pcb->state to CLOSED here or we risk leaking segments */
         recv_flags |= TF_CLOSED;
+        /* This ACK only acknowledges our own FIN (tcp_seg::len is always 0
+           for a FIN-only segment, see tcp_enqueue_flags()), so recv_acked
+           stays 0 and the TCP_EVENT_SENT sent-callback below is never
+           invoked. Without an explicit poll here, a netconn waiting on
+           lwip_netconn_do_close_internal() (registered via tcp_poll() while
+           closing was still pending) is never given a chance to notice
+           that the close has now completed before tcp_input_delayed_close()
+           frees this pcb, and the application's close() call hangs forever.
+           Mirror the TCP_POLL_LINGERING_CLOSE(pcb) call already used for
+           the equivalent FIN_WAIT_1/FIN_WAIT_2/CLOSING -> TIME_WAIT
+           transitions above. */
+        TCP_POLL_LINGERING_CLOSE(pcb);
       }
       break;
     default:
